\details{
The plots and statistics depend on the combination of variable types:\tabular{ll}{
   Combination \tab Output \cr
   nominal / ordinal \tab bar chart and frequency table \cr
   discrete \tab bar chart with one bar per value (histogram with classes if more than \code{max_bars} values are possible), mean and median lines, 1-D boxplot, statistics table \cr
   continuous \tab histogram with density curve, mean and median lines, 1-D boxplot, statistics table \cr
   numeric x numeric \tab scatter plot with linear regression (95\% CI) and LOESS smoother, regression equation, R², Pearson's \emph{r} with p-value, marginal distributions, statistics table \cr
   categorical x numeric \tab boxplot with raw data and mean ± 95\% CI, violin plot, group means ± 95\% CI with grand mean, per-group statistics table \cr
   categorical x categorical \tab grouped bar chart, contingency table, chi-squared test of independence \cr
}


The statistics table contains N, mean, median, SD, variance, IQR, min/max,
skewness, kurtosis, SE of the mean, and the two-sided 95\% confidence
interval, which is always based on the \emph{t}-distribution with \emph{N} - 1 degrees
of freedom. The same statistics are returned as data by \code{\link[=describe_var]{describe_var()}}.

Bar charts of nominal variables are sorted by frequency, those of ordinal
variables by level order. Missing values are removed; for two variables,
only complete pairs are used, and the number of removed pairs is reported in
the subtitle.
