\section{Variable types}{

R stores \emph{how} data are encoded (character, factor, integer, double), but
not \emph{what} the values mean. Hence, the scale of measurement cannot always be
derived from the data alone:
\itemize{
\item category codes stored as numbers (e.g., \code{1} = control, \code{2} = treatment)
look like discrete counts,
\item counts stored as \code{double} look like continuous measurements,
\item a character vector or unordered factor may in fact be ordinal.
}

If \code{x_type} and \code{y_type} are \code{NULL} (default), \code{explore_var()} therefore
follows simple, transparent rules based only on how a variable is stored in
R:\tabular{ll}{
   Encoding in R \tab Detected type \cr
   \code{character}, \code{logical}, unordered \code{factor} \tab nominal \cr
   ordered \code{factor} (see \code{\link[base:factor]{base::ordered()}}) \tab ordinal \cr
   \code{integer} \tab discrete \cr
   \code{double} \tab continuous \cr
}


The detected type is always reported in the console. In two ambiguous
situations, an additional hint suggests how to correct the type:
\itemize{
\item an \code{integer} variable with few distinct values (10 or fewer) may contain
category codes rather than counts,
\item a \code{double} variable containing only whole numbers may contain counts and
thus be discrete.
}
}

\section{Setting the type manually}{

If the automatic detection does not match the meaning of a variable, there
are two ways to correct it:
\enumerate{
\item \strong{Encode the variable correctly in R} (recommended). Category codes
become a factor, e.g., \code{factor(x, labels = c("control", "treatment"))};
ordinal data become an ordered factor, e.g.,
\code{factor(x, levels = c("low", "medium", "high"), ordered = TRUE)}; and
counts stored as \code{double} become integers with \code{as.integer(x)}.
\item \strong{Set the type explicitly} with \code{x_type} and \code{y_type}, e.g.,
\code{x_type = "discrete"} for counts stored as \code{double}, or
\code{x_type = "continuous"} for rounded measurements stored as \code{integer}
(such as temperatures in whole degrees). The variable is converted
internally; the original data remain unchanged.
}

If a non-factor vector is set to \code{"ordinal"}, its levels are sorted
numerically or alphabetically, and a hint shows the resulting order. Setting
\code{"discrete"} or \code{"continuous"} requires numeric values, and \code{"discrete"}
additionally requires whole numbers; otherwise, an error is raised.
}

